<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "https://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!--
  Entitlements for the bundled signal-cli, and for nothing else.

  signal-cli is a GraalVM native image that carries libsignal inside itself and
  unpacks it at runtime — it writes libsignal_jni_aarch64.dylib into a temporary
  directory and dlopens it. Under the hardened runtime, library validation
  refuses to load a dylib that is not signed by the same team, and the extracted
  copy is signed by nobody. signal-cli reports that as:

      Failed to call libsignal-client: Can't load library:
        /var/folders/…/libsignal_jni_aarch64.dylib
      Missing required native library dependency: libsignal-client

  and exits. Every attempt to link a phone therefore ended in "That didn't
  finish" with no QR code ever appearing.

  This is the failure the main entitlements file predicts and explains: an
  ad-hoc signature has no Team ID, so library validation is not enforced at all
  on a development build, and the whole class of bug is invisible until a
  Developer ID build runs on somebody's Mac.

  What that file gets wrong is where the fix belongs. Library validation governs
  code loaded into a process's own address space, and the process loading this
  library is signal-cli — not Mynah. Adding the key to Mynah's entitlements
  would weaken every future load in the GUI and would not have fixed this.

  So the entitlement lives here, applied to one helper, which is the smallest
  grant that makes linking work. Mynah's own entitlements are unchanged and
  still do not disable library validation.
-->
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
